Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 08:45:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 69/71] Add a read tool taking a list of paths (measured, not
 proven)

Reads go through the shell, so a batch has to be one chained command whose combined output is
capped, which keeps batches at three to six files. On the `wire` task that costs eight read turns
against the three an agent with a list-taking read tool needs, and every extra turn resends the
window.

`read({paths, offset?, limit?})` returns one section per file, enforces its own per-file line and
byte caps so one large file cannot consume the batch, and stops on a file boundary naming what it
did not reach rather than letting middle-out truncation cut through a file.

Measured on `wire`, three runs against a two-run baseline of 18/18 requests:

  baseline   18, 18            $0.01771
  with read  12, 17, 23        $0.01840

The mechanism works - the best run read 15 + 11 + 1 files in three turns and delivered 44/44 valid
citations. But the model is inconsistent: run three opened with 8, 8, 8 files and then degenerated
into single-file calls. Reading one file through the shell also cost a whole command, so the shell
forced batching; a cheap per-file tool removes that pressure. Mean is level with the baseline, cost
is slightly worse, variance triples.

Parked on this branch rather than merged. The identified next step is a description that refuses a
single-path call when several files are already known.

